WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:05.430 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  as i started speaking  what was the first thing you noticed  about me  was it my charming smile  my charismatic personality  or  was it my accent  having lived in the u.s now for almost  three years  i realized that for a lot of people it  is my accent  i come from pakistan  which is why i have an unmistakable  south asian accent  i've been fascinated by accidents for  many years  first because well we all have one  second  i feel strongly about how a person's  unique accent can trigger people's  conscious and unconscious biases  and lead to accident discrimination  research has shown that we are less  likely to believe something if it is  said with a foreign accent  these negative judgments are the result  of the additional effort that our brains  must make to process foreign speech  our brains then shift the blame for this  additional effort onto the truthfulness  of the speaker  and it can trigger frustration  about a year ago i was on the phone with  my health insurance company for some  information i needed before an upcoming  trip  i had been on the call for almost an  hour  hopping from one collagen to another  without any solution to my problem  i was becoming more and more impatient  i was then redirected for the fourth  time  and as the new college agent started  speaking  i instantly noticed a distinct east  asian accent  perhaps chinese or korean  and i exploded internally  i don't need this accent right now  i didn't say this to her of course  but that's what i thought  my frustration was caused by my complete  inability to understand anything she was  saying  and then i stopped  at that point it hit me  i was doing exactly what i've been so  strongly opposed to  i was allowing someone's accent  to influence how i saw them  how i listened to them  how i treated them  that day  i was able to name what i felt deeply  about for many years  accent empathy  i define accent empathy as thinking  deeper  about how a person's unique accent is a  manifestation of their lived experience  of their cultural identity  of their humanity  growing up in pakistan i saw firsthand  how perfecting your english proficiency  was seen as much more than just language  acquisition  it was a symbol of status of ability of  sophistication  this is the legacy of 200 years of  british colonialism  mastery of spoken english was what  separated the colonial masters from  their indian subjects  this gave rise to the brown sahabs  indians who yearned to imitate the  british in language stress or lifestyle  because doing so conferred patronage  from the colonial government  even years after the british left the  indian subcontinent  this english-centered indoctrination was  still alive and well  i grew up seeing adults and children  giggle at and even openly insult people  who spoke broken english or  mispronounced an english word  and the opposite was also true  speaking fluent english with perfect  pronunciation and good accents gave you  prestige  high school debate coach once said to  our entire team  all of you need to fix your accent  except mansoor  i'll be like if i said i didn't feel  immense pride at that point  it may seem to be misplaced pride in  retrospect  but then  in that moment  my pride was real  yet this reinforced within me a certain  toxic complex  and insecurity  i had to protect my accent  as i traveled to places outside of  pakistan  i began to realize that  mastery of a language or accent is not a  sign of competence  i began to accept my accent as part of  who i am  of my identity  then i came to the u.s  and suddenly found myself in the odd  position of the guy with the different  foreign accent  sometimes  when i spoke in zoo meetings  i was met with complete silence  the group  all native english speakers  would move on to speaking amongst  themselves  why was this happening  did they disagree with the substance of  what i'd said  was it zoom fatigue  was it my accent  i could only speculate  but this did take a toll on me  i felt overlooked  dismissed  invisible  fortunately for me by this stage of my  life i genuinely began to cherish my  unique accent  i'm not making a political statement of  any sort  i truly cherish my pakistani accent as a  symbol of my life journey and my  identity  i value it as an expression of love  for my family  my grandparents  my home  but accident discrimination is real  according to research at curtin  university in australia  there's a hierarchy that places american  british and australian english accents  at the top  views european accented english as cute  or stylish  but asian or african accents as ugly and  unpleasant  the uk  is known to view specific accents as  working class versus sophisticated  former british prime minister margaret  thatcher had to lose the lincolnshire  accent to sound more posh  in the u.s the story is a bit more  nuanced  laura rivera in her book pedigree  writes that accents play a role in  shaping recruitment at elite firms  according to rivera  interviewers frequently questioned  the polish of foreign students of all  races  complaining  that most candidates actions made them  difficult to understand  exceptions  were those with pure british  german or scandinavian accents  this is the accent hierarchy at work  there's also anti-black racism in  linguistics  something that even former first lady  michelle obama had to contend with  and she's a harvard educated lawyer  but i believe that we owe it to  ourselves  and to each other  to put in that little extra effort  to move beyond any cognitive and  intellectual laziness that might be  stifling us  so the next time you find yourself  struggling to engage with someone having  an unfamiliar accent  i challenge you to pause  take a deep breath  ask yourself why am i having such a  reflexive reaction  is it just because the accident is  different from my own  think about what your accent says about  you  your family your festivals  your aspirations your struggles  your identity  do you really want to rob someone of the  same  just so you can save a few seconds of  mental energy  that  to me is accent empathy in action  and if we truly aspire to change lives  and change organizations in an  increasingly interconnected world we  need access empathy  thank you  [Music]  you

